# Syntax: docs/LANGUAGE.md. Check with `rbb bid check`.
#
# No auction patterns here. These rules key off auction state that other
# modules set (trump agreed, game forcing), so they work after any auction
# that reaches that state.

module rkcb-1430 "Roman Keycard Blackwood (1430; 0314 as a treatment)"
  needs  base
  skill  bidding_conventions/roman_keycard
  skill  partnership_bidding/roman_key_card
  param  k1430 = slam.blackwood.rkcb_1430 default false
  param  k0314 = slam.blackwood.rkcb_0314 default false
  param  m0314 = slam.blackwood.minor_0314 default false
  param  gstyle = general.style
  param  style = slam.cue_bids.style default first_or_second_round
  param  kask5n = slam.king_ask.five_nt default false

# ── The ask ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Rick's ruling:
#
#   Slam asks isn't about 18 -- what has partner shown? It's more about
#   33+ combined points, where in a suit contract the player with fewer
#   trumps gets to switch to short-suit points -- 1 for a doubleton, 3
#   for a singleton, 5 for a void, capped by number of trumps held. When
#   the slam asker has unprotected suits, we will normally use control
#   bids before keycard, and stop in game if there is a suit unprotected
#   in both hands.
#
# So the test is **33 combined support points with the agreed suit as
# trump**, `we.tp(trump)`: my exact count plus the range a raise recorded
# for partner. It replaces `tp(trump) + partner.hcp.max`, which was a
# workaround from before raises showed support points. The evidence
# behind the boundaries is in slam-catch.notes.md.
#
# Two branches, because partner's range is read from a different end.
#
# 1. **Partner is limited** (a raise, a limit raise) and nothing has
#    forced us to game. Partner has shown a top as well as a bottom, and
#    the only other call on offer is game, so the ask carries the whole
#    decision and 33 is measured against partner's maximum:
#    `we.tp(trump).max >= 33`. With it, the hand must play for twelve
#    tricks opposite a minimum (`losers <= 4`) and must not have **two
#    bare side suits** -- two or more cards with neither the ace nor the
#    king, where the first two tricks can go. That last test replaces
#    `controls >= 7`: it says which suit is the problem rather than
#    counting. One bare suit is allowed, because a raise facing it will
#    usually hold something there; two is where partner cannot cover
#    both. BBA draws the same line: over `1S P 2S P` and `1S P 3S P` on
#    the Basic-Bridge card it asks with none or one bare suit (272
#    boards) and never with two.
#
# 2. **We are already forced to game.** Partner's range has no top, so
#    `we.tp(trump).max` says nothing; what is known is partner's floor.
#    33 between us on that floor is the test, and where the trump
#    agreement recorded no support points for partner the fallback is my
#    own 18, a game force being worth about 13 (`slam_values`, named in
#    slam-entry.bid with the other shared conditions). Here the
#    ask ranks *behind* the system's descriptive calls: shortness, a
#    second suit and a control bid all say more than 4NT does, and the
#    ask is what is left when they are used up.
#
# When the card plays control bids (`slam.cue_bids.play`), a hand with a
# bare suit goes to `control-bids.bid` first and comes back here only
# once partner has shown a control.

when (k1430 | k0314), we.trump is suit, !minor_given_way, !asked, we.forcing = none | we.forcing = round, !kb_asks(trump), !mw_asks(trump)   # Kickback, Minorwood: their own ask
  4N  "Keycard ask in {trump}"
      when  slam_values_limited     # 33 facing partner's maximum
      when  !two_bare_suits  # and no two bare side suits
      sets  ask=keycards(trump)
      artificial
      priority 1

when (k1430 | k0314), we.trump is suit, !minor_given_way, !asked, we.forcing = game, !answered control, !kb_asks(trump), !mw_asks(trump)   # after a control dialogue its own rules decide
  4N  "Keycard ask in {trump}"
      when  slam_values  # 33 on partner's floor, or 18 of my own
      sets  ask=keycards(trump)
      artificial

# A fit known but not agreed (docs/JUDGMENT-LAYER.md, Phase 1): partner's
# game raise, his preempt, a transfer completed. No rule set `trump`, so
# the asks above never saw it. The ask agrees the major on the way.
# Majors only for now: the sign-offs after an answer are awkward in a
# minor.
when (k1430 | k0314), we.trump is none, !asked, !they.bid   # uncontested for now: in competition it cost
  4N  "Keycard ask, agreeing ♠"
      when  shown_fit(S), we.fit(S).min >= 8, fit_slam_values(S), !two_bare_beside(S)
      sets  trump=S, ask=keycards(S)
      artificial
      priority 1
  4N  "Keycard ask, agreeing ♥"
      when  shown_fit(H), we.fit(H).min >= 8, fit_slam_values(H), !two_bare_beside(H)
      sets  trump=H, ask=keycards(H)
      artificial
      priority 1

# The keycard suit when no rule has set the one we play in (Rick,
# 2026-10-03; `kc_open`, slam-entry.bid): a suit agreed by what we have
# shown, else, with no notrump bid by us, the last suit we bid
# (1H-1S-2H-4NT asks in hearts). The ask sets it as trump. The majors
# agreed publicly, and partner's own six-card major, are the rules above.
# The ask needs the fit and the values, as above.
when (k1430 | k0314), we.trump is none, !asked, !they.bid
  4N  "Keycard ask in ♠: the last suit we bid"
      when  kc_open(S), !shown_fit(S), !shown_fit(H), we.fit(S).min >= 8, fit_slam_values(S), !two_bare_beside(S)
      sets  trump=S, ask=keycards(S)
      artificial
      priority -1
  4N  "Keycard ask in ♥: the last suit we bid"
      when  kc_open(H), !shown_fit(S), !shown_fit(H), we.fit(H).min >= 8, fit_slam_values(H), !two_bare_beside(H)
      sets  trump=H, ask=keycards(H)
      artificial
      priority -1
  4N  "Keycard ask in ♦"
      when  kc_open(D), !shown_fit(S), !shown_fit(H), we.fit(D).min >= 8, fit_slam_values(D), !two_bare_beside(D)
      when  hcp + partner.hcp.min >= 30   # a minor slam needs the high cards (rebids.test: 17 facing a preference)
      sets  trump=D, ask=keycards(D)
      artificial
      priority -1
  4N  "Keycard ask in ♣"
      when  kc_open(C), !shown_fit(S), !shown_fit(H), we.fit(C).min >= 8, fit_slam_values(C), !two_bare_beside(C)
      when  hcp + partner.hcp.min >= 30   # a minor slam needs the high cards (rebids.test: 17 facing a preference)
      sets  trump=C, ask=keycards(C)
      artificial
      priority -1

# The same 4NT in competition, for reading only (2026-10-05, DOPI and
# ROPI): the asks above are off once they have bid, but partner's 4NT is
# still keycard for the suit we play in, and the answer comes by DOPI or
# ROPI (dopi-ropi.bid). Never chosen: `read_only` is a condition on the
# caller's hand that no hand meets, and that a reader cannot judge, so
# it stays possible when the call is read (docs/LANGUAGE.md, section 8).
# Low priority as well, so any other meaning of 4NT comes first.
define read_only = bare(C), !bare(C)

when (k1430 | k0314), we.trump is none, !asked, they.bid
  4N  "Keycard ask in ♠"
      when  kc_open(S) | shown_fit(S)
      sets  trump=S, ask=keycards(S)
      artificial
      when  read_only
      priority -60
  4N  "Keycard ask in ♥"
      when  kc_open(H) | shown_fit(H), !shown_fit(S)
      sets  trump=H, ask=keycards(H)
      artificial
      when  read_only
      priority -60
  4N  "Keycard ask in ♦"
      when  kc_open(D), !shown_fit(S), !shown_fit(H)
      sets  trump=D, ask=keycards(D)
      artificial
      when  read_only
      priority -60
  4N  "Keycard ask in ♣"
      when  kc_open(C), !shown_fit(S), !shown_fit(H)
      sets  trump=C, ask=keycards(C)
      artificial
      when  read_only
      priority -60

# A trump suit agreed and partner's jump to game in competition
# (total-tricks.bid's `ask=signoff`, 1H-2NT-(3C)-4H): 4NT over the
# sign-off is still keycard. For reading, as above.
when (k1430 | k0314), we.trump is suit, they.bid, asked signoff
  4N  "Keycard ask in {trump}"
      sets  ask=keycards(trump)
      artificial
      when  read_only
      priority -60

# A minor set as trump, and a major agreed since (Rick, 2026-10-03:
# "normally a major fit will replace a minor fit"; 1S-2C-3C-4S-4NT): the
# ask is in the major, and makes it trump.
when (k1430 | k0314), minor_given_way, !asked
  4N  "Keycard ask in ♠: the major fit replaces the minor"
      when  major_over_minor(S), fit_slam_values(S), !two_bare_beside(S)
      sets  trump=S, ask=keycards(S)
      artificial
      priority 1
  4N  "Keycard ask in ♥: the major fit replaces the minor"
      when  major_over_minor(H), !major_over_minor(S), fit_slam_values(H), !two_bare_beside(H)
      sets  trump=H, ask=keycards(H)
      artificial
      priority 1

# After a control-bid exchange (`control-bids.bid`, when the card plays
# them). `asked control(t)` is partner's control bid; `answered control(u)`
# says partner was answering mine, so I am the hand that opened the
# dialogue and the one that takes charge. Above it, the stop-in-game rule
# at priority 20 has already ruled out a suit bare in both hands; below
# it, any control still to show outranks the ask.

# In a control-bid dialogue (control-bids.bid): ask once every side suit
# is covered -- a control of mine, one partner has cued, or one I denied
# that partner has promised by bidding on. A control bid below game still
# comes first (priority 12), the ask before one past game (10).
when (k1430 | k0314), we.trump is suit, asked control(t), !kb_asks(trump)
  4N  "Keycard ask in {trump}"
      when  all_covered
      sets  ask=keycards(trump)
      artificial
      priority 11
  # Partner's control bid has passed 4NT (a first-round control past
  # game, as over a non-serious 3NT, serious-3nt.bid): with every suit
  # covered and the values, bid the slam rather than go on cueing and
  # stop in five (2026-10-05, Serious boards 17 and 47).
  6{trump}  "Slam: every suit covered, the keycard ask is gone"
      when  all_covered, slam_values, partner.last >= 5C
      priority 11

when (k1430 | k0314), we.trump is suit, answered control(t), partner.last = 4{trump}, !kb_asks(trump)
  4N  "Keycard ask in {trump}"
      when  all_covered_by_us
      sets  ask=keycards(trump)
      artificial
      priority 15

when (k1430 | k0314), asked keycards(t)
  # We play 1430 whatever the card says (Rick, 2026-09-27); the
  # `minor_0314` treatment swaps the first two answers when a minor is
  # trumps. BBA plays the card's 0314 (Precision, 21GF-GIB, 21GF-GIB-
  # Bergen), so `general.style=bba` does too.
  5C  "1 or 4 keycards"                shows keycards(t) in 1|4  artificial
      when  gstyle is not bba, !m0314 | t is H | t is S
  5D  "0 or 3 keycards"                shows keycards(t) in 0|3  artificial
      when  gstyle is not bba, !m0314 | t is H | t is S
  5C  "0 or 3 keycards"                shows keycards(t) in 0|3  artificial
      when  gstyle is not bba, m0314, t is C | t is D
  5D  "1 or 4 keycards"                shows keycards(t) in 1|4  artificial
      when  gstyle is not bba, m0314, t is C | t is D
  5C  "1 or 4 keycards"                shows keycards(t) in 1|4  artificial
      when  gstyle is bba, !k0314
  5D  "0 or 3 keycards"                shows keycards(t) in 0|3  artificial
      when  gstyle is bba, !k0314
  5C  "0 or 3 keycards"                shows keycards(t) in 0|3  artificial
      when  gstyle is bba, k0314
  5D  "1 or 4 keycards"                shows keycards(t) in 1|4  artificial
      when  gstyle is bba, k0314
  # Five keycards answer as two: the steps wrap (0/3, 1/4, 2/5), and the
  # asker, holding none, knows which (Benjamin_2D 222, 2026-10-07: we
  # passed the ask with five).
  5H  "2 or 5 keycards, no {t} queen"     shows keycards(t) in 2|5, !has(Q,t)  artificial
  5S  "2 or 5 keycards and the {t} queen" shows keycards(t) in 2|5, has(Q,t)  artificial

# The asker places the contract. we.keycards(t) combines my exact count
# with partner's answer; the deck limit of 5 keycards resolves "1 or 4".
when (k1430 | k0314), answered keycards(t)
  7{t}  "Grand slam"
        when we.keycards(t)=5, has(Q,t) | partner.has(Q,t) | partner.last = 5S, grand_try, !kask5n   # with the king ask, ask first (2026-10-07)
  # The king ask (`slam.king_ask.five_nt`, 2026-10-05): every keycard
  # and the queen are ours, and the grand turns on the side kings.
  5N    "King ask: all the keycards and the {t} queen are ours, how many side kings?"
        when kask5n, we.keycards(t)=5, has(Q,t) | partner.has(Q,t) | partner.last = 5S
        sets ask=kings(t)
        artificial
  6{t}  "Small slam: at most one keycard missing"
        when we.keycards(t)>=4
  # The count that fits (2026-10-07): with 30 HCP between us the low
  # count of an open answer is impossible (`kc_high`, slam-entry.bid),
  # so no sign-off: on with all five and the queen, else six.
  7{t}  "Grand slam: the high count is the one that fits, all five and the {t} queen"
        when kc_high(t), we.keycards(t).max=5, has(Q,t) | partner.last = 5S, grand_try, !kask5n   # with the king ask, ask first (2026-10-07)
  5N    "King ask: the high count fits, all the keycards and the {t} queen are ours"
        when kask5n, kc_high(t), we.keycards(t).max=5, has(Q,t) | partner.last = 5S
        sets ask=kings(t)
        artificial
  6{t}  "Small slam: the high count is the one that fits"
        when kc_high(t)
  P     "Sign off: partner's answer is in our suit"
        when partner.last=5{t}, we.keycards(t).max<=3
  5{t}  "Sign off: two keycards missing"
        when we.keycards(t).max<=3     # filtered out as illegal when the answer
                                       # is already 5{t}; P above covers that
        sets ask=keycard_correct(t)    # partner corrects only with 3-4
  5{t}  "Sign off: partner corrects to 6 with the higher count"
        when maybe we.keycards(t)>=4   # 0 or 3 / 1 or 4 still open
        sets ask=keycard_correct(t)
  # With clubs as trumps every answer but 5♣ passes the sign-off
  # (2026-10-05: we passed partner's artificial 5♦ on ~30 boards). Six
  # then: the answer is never a place to play, and an open count ("0 or
  # 3") is far more often the higher one opposite slam values.
  6{t}  "No sign-off below six: the answer has passed five of our suit"
        priority -1

# The king ask by 5NT: the number of kings outside the trump suit (its
# king is a keycard), by steps from 6♣. As ACBL's SAYC booklet and BBA
# (6♣ 0, 6♦ 1, 6♥ 2, 6♠ 3). Kings are controls less two for each ace.
define side_kings(t) = controls - keycards(N) - keycards(N) - has(K,t)

when kask5n, asked kings(t)
  6C  "No side king"     shows side_kings(t) = 0   artificial
  6D  "One side king"    shows side_kings(t) = 1   artificial
  6H  "Two side kings"   shows side_kings(t) = 2   artificial
  6S  "Three side kings" shows side_kings(t) = 3   artificial

# The asker: the grand with every side king between us, or with two, 33
# HCP on partner's floor and a hand that can take care of the missing
# king's suit (a singleton or void, or six trumps in either hand); else six, or 6NT when
# the answer has passed six of a minor. With a flat hand the missing king
# is a finesse for the grand: on Puppet Stayman's 4-4 fits it went down
# as often as it made (2026-10-07, rkcb-1430.notes.md).
define partner_side_kings = (partner.last = 6D) + (partner.last = 6H) + (partner.last = 6H) + (partner.last = 6S) + (partner.last = 6S) + (partner.last = 6S)
define our_side_kings(t) = side_kings(t) + partner_side_kings

when kask5n, answered kings(t)
  7{t}  "Grand slam: every side king is ours"
        when our_side_kings(t) >= 3
  7{t}  "Grand slam: two side kings, 33 HCP between us, and shortness or six trumps"
        when our_side_kings(t) >= 2, hcp + partner.hcp.min >= 33, C<=1 | D<=1 | H<=1 | S<=1 | t>=6 | partner.t.min>=6
  P     "Small slam: partner's answer is our suit"
        when partner.last = 6{t}
  6{t}  "Small slam: a side king missing"
  6N    "Small slam: the answer has passed six of our suit"

# When the answer was ambiguous the asker signs off in five, and the answerer
# corrects to six holding the higher count (3 or 4 keycards).
when (k1430 | k0314), asked keycard_correct(t)
  6{t}  "Corrects to six: the higher count"   shows keycards(t) in 3|4|5
  P     "Accepts the sign-off"

# TODO: queen ask (next step after 5C/5D), specific-king ask at 5NT.
